<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?><kml xmlns="http://www.opengis.net/kml/2.2"><Document><name>ILLUSTRATIVE progress-ms-36 ascent</name><description>FLOWN 2026-11-24: T0 is the actual liftoff 2026-11-24T10:17:46Z (+286 s from the tentative 2026-11-24T10:13:00Z). LL2 id 8dd5de11-831b-445f-86f0-bffefe0550d9, Launch Successful, second precision, last_updated 2026-11-24T10:35:14Z, window start and end both 10:17:46Z. Vehicle Soyuz-2.1b. TV Zvezda, reporting the Roscosmos broadcast: Soyuz-2.1b with Progress MS-36 launched from Baikonur (https://tvzvezda.ru/news/202611241319-gfXuX.html). Lenta quotes Roscosmos: insertion onto the assigned orbit, separation from the third stage, and deployment of the antennas and solar arrays were nominal (https://lenta.ru/news/2026/11/24/raketa-s-teledroidom-startovala/). utc shifted +286 s. ECEF geometry unchanged. Sim not re-run. Knots not moved. ILLUSTRATIVE.
ILLUSTRATIVE ASCENT ECEF — Soyuz-2.1b Progress MS-36 (97P, Baikonur 31/6) — NOT OFFICIAL TELEMETRY
Stage-3 clock: this rhumb stops at core separation. The sim flies Block I for Wikipedia's 270 s RD-0124 burn (cutoff T+556.159 s) and separates the ship 4 s later (T+560.159 s). The Soyuz-2.1a cutoff 525.45 s and the 8 min 49 s separation are recorded and are not these hard events.
PRE-FLIGHT. T0 2026-11-24T10:13:00Z (LL2 id 8dd5de11-831b-445f-86f0-bffefe0550d9, status TBC, last_updated 2026-11-22T19:47:17Z, net precision Minute, update 'Updated tentative T-0'). RETIMED +18600 s from 05:03Z. SOURCE CONFLICT: no Roscosmos confirmation. RussianSpaceWeb's post is what LL2 cites. Stale mirrors still show 05:03Z.
VEHICLE CONFLICT: this file uses Soyuz-2.1b. LL2 and Next Spaceflight say 2.1b. Interfax (22 September, year not printed) quotes Roscosmos that 2.1b was adapted for Progress and first flew one on 16 September, while 2.1a continues for crew and satellites. Roscosmos TV lists the launch and does not name the variant. Wikipedia EN says Soyuz-2.1a. Event times below are borrowed from Soyuz-2.1a Progress flights.
Knots borrowed from Progress MS-19 / MS-27 (RussianSpaceWeb), not this flight: booster sep 117.848 s / ~43 km; fairing 183.079 s / ~91 km / ~200 km downrange; core sep 287.419 s / ~143 km / ~500 km downrange.
  Booster-sep range is ASSUMED: 200 km * 117.848/183.079 = 128.7 km, linear in time from liftoff to the fairing knot. Altitude and range are linear between knots.
FILE ENDS AT CORE SEPARATION (T+287.419 s). Third-stage flight and spacecraft separation are in the sim file. A rhumb through the borrowed insertion would end within a few seconds of separation.
Azimuth 63.39 deg ASSUMED: non-rotating northeast heading for 51.6 deg from this pad, sin(az)=cos(51.6)/cos(45.996034). No hazard polygon found. Not retuned.
Horizontal speed is d(range)/dt of that linear range, plus vertical rate. Not a published speed.
Pad (LL2): 45.996034N 63.564003E, elevation ASSUMED 0 m. Frame: WGS-84 ECEF, positions km, velocities km/s.
Constant-azimuth rhumb Az=63.39 deg from pad (spherical R=6371.0088 km).
T0 used: 2026-11-24T10:17:46Z. Re-run with another T0 by shifting utc_iso only; ECEF geometry is Earth-fixed.</description>
